WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:05.083
The attached piece can regrow.

2
00:00:05.823 --> 00:00:07.983
So why does it stall in the brain and spinal cord?

3
00:00:09.017 --> 00:00:13.857
In 1981, Samuel David and Albert Aguayo changed mainly the tissue.

4
00:00:14.777 --> 00:00:18.817
In adult rats, they bridged the medulla to the spinal cord with peripheral nerve.

5
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.100
If adult central neurons cannot grow long axons at all, nothing grows.

6
00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.040
If the tissue stops them, what should you see?

7
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:37.733
Central axons grew about 30 millimetres along the grafts.

8
00:00:37.993 --> 00:00:40.113
Back in central tissue, they soon stopped.

9
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.640
Now keep the central tissue, and change mainly the neuron.

10
00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:53.360
In 2008, Zhigang He's laboratory deleted PTEN from retinal ganglion cells in mice.

11
00:00:54.383 --> 00:00:56.523
PTEN is a brake on cell growth.

12
00:00:57.343 --> 00:01:00.083
Then they crushed the optic nerve, which is central tissue.

13
00:01:00.963 --> 00:01:01.983
Will the axons cross?

14
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:13.507
Some do. They come from a minority of the neurons, but far more than normal.

15
00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:15.850
So it is not a gate.

16
00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:19.610
Each experiment got growth while changing mainly one side.

17
00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:21.450
It is a balance.

18
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:26.000
Picture neurons spread out by drive, and a line set by the tissue.

19
00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.600
Only neurons past the line regrow.

20
00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.610
Lower the line, or push the neurons up, and some get through.

21
00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.090
Here the line sits in the thin tail.

22
00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:38.470
So doing both passes more than the two gains added.

23
00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:41.670
With another spread, the gains might only add.

24
00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:43.950
This is a model, not data.

25
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:48.313
Myelin proteins such as Nogo collapse growth cones.

26
00:01:49.113 --> 00:01:51.573
Yet mice lacking three of them barely regrow more.

27
00:01:52.333 --> 00:01:53.333
Why?

28
00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:02.647
One way to see it: most neurons may sit far below the line.

29
00:02:03.707 --> 00:02:05.167
Lowering it a little moves few across.

30
00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:07.447
Other barriers remain, too.

31
00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:10.487
The scar resists, but also helps.

32
00:02:11.367 --> 00:02:15.307
In mice, removing its astrocyte border reduced stimulated regrowth.

33
00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:17.827
Change the scar, do not remove it.

34
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.620
And regrowth is not recovery.

35
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:25.840
At a steady millimetre a day, a metre takes about a thousand days, nearly three

36
00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:31.200
years. Over that long a time, Schwann cells lose their repair state, and distant

37
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.440
targets are hard to reach.

38
00:02:35.817 --> 00:02:39.617
Blocking SARM1 may help failing axons that are still attached.

39
00:02:40.517 --> 00:02:43.937
A fully severed stump cannot rejoin its cell body on its own.

40
00:02:44.867 --> 00:02:47.227
For regrowth, no single fix is enough.

41
00:02:48.227 --> 00:02:50.467
None yet restores function reliably in people.

42
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.000
One cut, two fates.

43
00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:58.280
A program for the cut-off piece, and a balance of drive against resistance for the

44
00:02:58.280 --> 00:02:58.960
attached one.
